---
name: smoke-ci
version: 1.0.0
---

# smoke-ci — the fast repo-wide suite

This is the suite that runs on **every push**, locally (`scripts/smoke-ci`, from the pre-push hook)
and on GitHub (`.github/workflows/smoke.yml`) — the same runner, the same checks, in that order.
The full suite still exists; it moved off the push path to `scripts/full-ci` and the nightly.

## Why the push path needed to shrink

Measured on the last `ci.yml` runs that reached completion: **13.4 min wall clock, ~64 runner-
minutes** per push, against a `main` push interval of ~3–7 min. GitHub keeps one pending run per
concurrency group, so most commits were superseded before they were ever tested — of the last 100
`ci.yml` runs, 83 `cancelled`, 4 `failure`, 0 `success`. That is not a slow gate, it is an absent
one, and `cancelled` reads as RED, so the apparatus was also manufacturing reds for commits that
were fine.

A suite that finishes inside the push interval gives a verdict on **every** commit. That is worth
more than a suite that covers everything and is read for none of them.

## What is in it, and why these

Two axes, both deliberate:

- **Breadth** — one check per module of the hierarchy in `tests/fixtures/modules.tsv`, so no module
  can go dark. The module list is READ from that file, not restated here: a check naming a module
  that does not exist is a hard error, which is what keeps this file from becoming a second copy of
  the hierarchy.
- **Criticality** — within a module, the checks that have actually caught something. Every gate
  below is here because it once failed on a real defect, not because it was cheap to add.

The corpus part is TWO `run.sc` invocations, split by lane (see the code comment for the
measurement that forced the split), over cases chosen the same way: `char-literal-escapes`
(a Char pattern that matched nothing and silently froze a parser corpus case), `arithmetic` and
`int-width` (Int is 64-bit and an overflow once failed open), `effects` (the one case that declares
all four backends, so a single case exercises int/js/jvm/v2), `std-process-import` (the native tier
had no `exec` at all until 2026-07-30), and one representative per subproject.

## Why this runner is written in `.ssc`

It runs on the v2 native lane, so the compiler under test is what executes the suite. That is not
decoration: writing it is what surfaced the missing `exec` native — `std/process.ssc` declares the
extern and int, js, jvm and rust all implemented it, so every other lane could shell out and the
DEFAULT one could not. A CI runner is subprocesses and nothing else, so the gap had nowhere to hide.

## Accounting

Per-check timings, aggregated per module, with a hard total budget (`SSC_SMOKE_BUDGET`, default
420 s). The budget is enforced, not documented — this file is where the push path stops being allowed
to grow silently, and a run that overruns it fails even when every check passed.

**420 s is sized on a DISTRIBUTION, not on one sample, and getting that wrong cost two red runs.**

The history is worth keeping, because each number was honestly derived and two of them were still
wrong:

| | suite | note |
|---|---|---|
| 300 s | 378.5 s | guessed from local timings; RED. A hosted runner has fewer, slower cores — the corpus slice was 233.9 s there against 68 s locally |
| 330 s | 250.2 s | after `SSC_CONF_WARM_JVM=1` cut the slice to 115.5 s; GREEN, and the cap was set from that single observation |
| 330 s | 338.6 s | 18/18 green, RED on the budget alone. The slice was 175.2 s that run — the SAME suite, +60 s |

The third row is the lesson: the dominant check varies by ±25 % between runs on a shared runner, so a
cap fitted to one sample encodes that sample's luck. POLICY P-6.6 says one measurement is a
hypothesis; a budget is a measurement like any other. 420 s sits above the observed maximum with room
for the spread, which is what lets it keep doing its actual job — catching STRUCTURAL growth, a check
someone added or a check that got slower — instead of flapping on runner noise.

When a run overruns THIS, read the per-check timings before touching the number; that is the whole
point of printing them. Uniform inflation across every check is a loaded host, not a slow suite. One
check that grew is the signal this budget exists for.

Both corpus checks' results are attributed per LANE and folded into the module totals through the
`lanes` column of `tests/fixtures/modules.tsv`, so `int`/`js`/`jvm`/`v2` land on the modules that
own them rather than all on `tests/conformance`. The rollup reads EVERY `corpus-*` check, not the
first one — reading only one would drop a whole lane while still printing a confident table.

```scalascript
[exec, ProcessOptions, ProcessResult](std/process.ssc)
[readFile](std/fs.ssc)
[args, envOrElse, exit](std/os.ssc)

// ── The suite ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `module` MUST be a row in tests/fixtures/modules.tsv. `timeoutMs` is a runaway guard, not an
// expectation: a check that approaches it is a check to look at, never a number to raise.

case class Check(module: String, name: String, cmd: String, argv: List[String], timeoutMs: Int,
                 env: Map[String, String] = Map(), optional: Boolean = false)

// ── OPTIONAL checks: off by default, and the default is the point ──────────────────────────────
//
// Some checks are worth having and are NOT worth failing a push over. Two kinds so far:
//
//   * a check that pins behaviour we have decided NOT to change yet. It asserts what is true today
//     rather than what is right, which makes it a tripwire for a future flip — and a tripwire in the
//     default run reads as "this is the contract". The next person to fix the behaviour would find a
//     test DEMANDING the broken version and reasonably conclude it was deliberate.
//   * a check that depends on something outside the commit — the network, a remote's state, an
//     authenticated CLI. It can go red for reasons the author of the push cannot fix, and a gate
//     that fails for reasons unrelated to your change is one people learn to re-run rather than read.
//
// They run with `SSC_SMOKE_OPTIONAL=1`. What they must NEVER do is disappear quietly: every run
// prints them by name with the reason they were skipped, and the summary says how many. A suite that
// silently drops checks is the "green because it cannot see" failure with extra steps — the
// difference between an optional check and a deleted one has to be visible in the log.
val runOptional: Boolean = envOrElse("SSC_SMOKE_OPTIONAL", "") != ""

// Cases are comma-separated globs; keep these lists short and keep every entry justified in the
// prose above — an unexplained case is one nobody will dare delete later. A name matching no case is
// an ERROR in run.sc, not a silently smaller run.
//
// TWO invocations, split by LANE, because one lane cost more than everything else put together.
// MEASURED on this slice, warm JVM, three alternating rounds: all four lanes 33.7-37.6 s, the same
// cases without `jvm` 11.9-15.8 s. The JVM lane was ~65 % of the check and ~half of the whole suite.
//
// The split is NOT "drop the JVM lane" — no lane goes dark on the push path. It is: stop paying the
// JVM lane ELEVEN times for breadth it does not add. Eight of these cases carry no `backends:` line
// at all, so they ran every lane by DEFAULT rather than by anyone's decision; the three below are
// the ones whose authors explicitly wrote `jvm` in their front-matter, i.e. where the JVM backend is
// the point. 12.1 + 6.8 = 18.9 s against 33.7 s.
//
// THE COST, stated: a JVM-lane regression in one of the other ten cases now surfaces on the PR or
// 4-hourly run instead of on the push. The full 363-case corpus still runs all four lanes there.
val corpusBreadth =
  "arithmetic,int-width,char-literal-escapes,pattern-matching,collections,strings," +
  "higher-order-functions,effects,tagless-program,json-value,std-process-import," +
  "js-int-division-by-zero,scljet-sql-select"

// `int-width` is here for a second reason: it is the ONLY case in the breadth list carrying
// `also-codegen: v2`, which is what produces the `JVM/v2` sub-lane (the v2 front emitting JVM
// bytecode). Splitting the lanes without it dropped that sub-lane from the push path entirely —
// caught by the per-module rollup showing v2 fall 4 -> 3, which is exactly the job that rollup has.
val corpusJvm = "effects,js-int-division-by-zero,try-catch-exception-delivery,int-width"

val suite = List(
  // ── `.` — cross-module: the freezes and process rules every other gate leans on.
  Check(".", "freeze-consistency", "tests/e2e/freeze-consistency-gate.sh", List(), 120000),
  Check(".", "policy-single-source", "tests/e2e/policy-single-source.sh", List("--self-test"), 120000),
  Check(".", "bugs-index", "tests/e2e/bugs-index-gate.sh", List("--self-test"), 120000),
  Check(".", "task-views", "tests/e2e/task-views-gate.sh", List("--self-test"), 120000),
  // One NUL byte makes `grep` treat a whole file as binary, and grep then prints nothing — the
  // same output it prints for "no matches". Seven tracked sources carried one (a Char sentinel
  // typed as a raw byte); the largest was the 227 KB v2 runtime, silently un-greppable for
  // anyone who did not pass `-a`. Seconds to run, and it keeps the tools honest about the
  // difference between "no" and "cannot answer".
  Check(".", "no-nul-in-sources", "tests/e2e/no-nul-in-sources.sh", List("--self-test"), 60000),
  Check(".", "frontmatter", "scala-cli",
        List("--server=false", "./v1/tools/scripts/validate-frontmatter.scala"), 300000),

  // ── tests — the apparatus. A sharding scheme that drops cases fails GREEN, so it is checked
  //    here, where `run.sc --list` enumerates the corpus without needing a built launcher.
  Check("tests", "conformance-shards-partition", "tests/e2e/build-conformance-shard-gate.sh", List("4"), 180000),
  Check("tests", "silent-assertions-frozen", "tests/e2e/silent-assertion-gate.sh", List(), 120000),
  // Two consumers, one fixture: the manifest's owner (v21-explicit-lanes-gate) and the reader that
  // only looks up a lane (bc-parity-sweep). The reader kept a second copy of the contract frozen at
  // a row count, the owner was maintained, and on 2026-07-31 they disagreed — the `sbt` job died at
  // exit 2 on a fixture the owner had certified green 34 min earlier in the same job. Seconds here;
  // the alternative is finding it six minutes into a build.
  Check("tests", "negtc-manifest-contract", "tests/e2e/negtc-manifest-contract-gate.sh", List(), 120000),
  // `--no-sweeps` DELIBERATELY, and the numbers are the argument. Whole gate 79 s, of which the
  // byte-equality half is 78 (real bc-parity sweeps over an `a*` slice) and the merge invariants
  // plus the "does ci.yml's own command line parse" check are 1. This suite was already measured at
  // 359 s against a 420 s cap with every check green (SPRINT `smoke-budget-drift`), so the 1 s half
  // goes here and the full gate runs in ci.yml's Validate job, off the push path.
  //
  // The 1 s half is the one that earns a push-path slot: it checks a workflow-to-script INTERFACE.
  // Four green 20-minute shards were discarded on run 30649090567 by a reduce job that died in 0 s
  // on argument ORDER, and no amount of corpus equality would have seen it.
  Check("tests", "negtc-mapreduce", "tests/e2e/negtc-mapreduce-gate.sh", List("--no-sweeps"), 60000),
  // Pure awk over a synthetic report, well under a second — and it had NEVER RUN. Nothing in this
  // repo invoked it, and its canonical fixture had drifted below the freeze's own floors, so under
  // `set -e` it exited at the first assertion and none of its seven drift rejections executed. The
  // freeze it guards is what decides whether a release is allowed, and on 2026-07-31 that freeze
  // failed a green corpus because a hardcoded 15 outlived the manifest it counted.
  Check("tests", "negtc-release-freeze", "tests/e2e/v21-negative-toolchain-release-gate-smoke.sh", List(), 60000),
  Check("tests", "ci-status-guard", "tests/e2e/ci-status-guard.sh", List(), 120000),
  Check("tests", "no-test-reaches-an-exiting-cli", "tests/e2e/cli-exit-reachability-guard.sh", List("--self-test"), 180000),
  // CI skips the ~3.5 min launcher build when this digest says the toolchain's inputs are unchanged.
  // If the digest ever stops noticing a compiler source, the suite runs on the PREVIOUS commit's
  // compiler and reports green about code it never executed — no symptom, better timings, wrong
  // verdict. That makes it a check the push path has to carry itself.
  Check("tests", "launcher-input-digest", "tests/e2e/launcher-digest-gate.sh", List(), 240000),

  // ── scripts — the coordination tooling parallel agents depend on being correct, plus the host
  //    RAM guards that stand between parallel agents and another OOM event.
  Check("scripts", "claim-mutex", "tests/coord/claim-mutex-conflict.sh", List(), 180000),
  Check("scripts", "claim-scope-hierarchy", "tests/coord/claim-scope-hierarchy.sh", List(), 180000),
  Check("scripts", "claim-hooks-refuse-overlap", "tests/coord/claim-hooks.sh", List(), 180000),
  Check("scripts", "board-generated", "tests/coord/board-generated.sh", List(), 180000),
  Check("scripts", "bench-wrapper-routing", "tests/e2e/bench-wrapper-gate.sh", List(), 120000),
  // The other half of the bench harness: `bench-wrapper-routing` checks that `scripts/bench` builds
  // the right sbt command; this one checks that `ssc bench`'s GENERATED wrapper actually measures.
  // It exists because the wrapper hardcoded a Long seed, so the one corpus row declaring
  // `def workload(seed: Int)` reported `n/a` on jvm AND js — read from the table, a wrapper defect
  // is indistinguishable from two backends that cannot run the workload. ~16 s, three lanes, two
  // seed types each; the Long fixture is the control that says the gate itself still works.
  // (BUGS.md bench-wrapper-hardcodes-a-long-seed.)
  Check("scripts", "bench-seed-type", "tests/e2e/bench-seed-type-gate.sh", List(), 180000),
  // `bugs-report` is the ONLY sanctioned way to ask what is open — grepping the prose is banned
  // because hand-rolled queries disagreed with each other. That makes its blind spots expensive:
  // 118 entries say `fixed` in their heading and `unknown` in their header, so the canonical
  // answer was low by 118 with nothing on screen to say so. The report now prints that deficit;
  // this keeps the DETECTOR alive (planted fixtures, not a frozen count, which every migration
  // would falsify). <1 s.
  Check("scripts", "bugs-status-drift", "tests/e2e/bugs-status-drift-gate.sh", List(), 60000),
  Check("scripts", "release-installer-launcher", "tests/e2e/release-installer-launcher.sh", List(), 180000),
  Check("scripts", "build-ram-guards-guard", "tests/e2e/build-ram-budget-gate.sh", List(), 180000),

  // ── three gates that existed and were run by nobody ────────────────────────────────────────────
  //
  // Each shipped with a working `--self-test` and then sat unwired, which is the same state as not
  // existing: an unrun gate is a hypothesis about the future, not a check on the present. MEASURED
  // before adding, because this suite's budget is the thing it exists to protect —
  // 128 + 72 + 782 ms plain (self-tests add 200 ms / 400 ms / 1.5 s), against a green run's
  // 259.3 s of 330 s. Roughly 1 s spent, ~68 s of headroom left.
  //
  // `--self-test` is on for the two that are cheap and OFF for the submodule one: its self-test
  // makes two extra network round-trips, and a push-path check should not spend a second proving
  // itself on every commit. Its refusal path is mutation-proven in the commit that added it (the
  // 2026-07-30 outage state reproduced, exit 1) — that evidence does not need re-earning per push.
  Check(".", "inbox-queue", "tests/e2e/inbox-gate.sh", List("--self-test"), 60000),
  Check("v1/runtime/backend/js", "json-number-policy-js", "tests/e2e/json-number-policy-js-gate.sh", List(), 60000),
  // JsGen has TWO function-emission paths and self-TCO was on only one of them, so the SAME
  // tail-recursive function looped at top level and recursed for real inside an `object` — a
  // difference no output comparison can see until a corpus case happens to get deep enough, which
  // is how it surfaced (scljet-large-page, `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded`). The
  // gate asserts BOTH placements and asserts the emitted LOOP, not only the answer: a future change
  // that dropped the transform and raised the stack instead would otherwise read as green.
  Check("v1/runtime/backend/js", "js-self-tco", "tests/e2e/js-self-tco-gate.sh", List(), 180000),
  // An intrinsic in the table but not in JsGen's chunk-trigger scan emits its CALL and none of its
  // DEFINITION — `ReferenceError` at runtime, nothing at compile time. THIRTEEN of the twenty-one
  // WsServer intrinsics were in that state on 2026-08-02 and only one had ever surfaced. Derived
  // from the intrinsic table and the chunk source rather than restated, so the next addition to
  // ws-server.mjs cannot quietly skip the list. Pure text analysis, no launcher, ~0 s.
  Check("v1/runtime/backend/js", "js-capability-triggers", "tests/e2e/js-capability-triggers-gate.sh", List(), 60000),
  // Tree-shaking runs on `emit-js`, NOT on the `run-js` the conformance runner uses, so a corpus
  // case cannot see this one: it printed the side effect before the fix and after it. The gate
  // emits the bundle and runs it, and also asserts that a genuinely PURE unused binding is still
  // dropped, so a "fix" that merely stopped shaking fails it. (js-unused-val-drops-side-effecting-call)
  Check("v1/runtime/backend/js", "js-shaker-effectful-binding",
        "tests/e2e/js-shaker-keeps-effectful-binding.sh", List(), 180000),
  // Runs BOTH v2 fronts and asserts `ssc info --front-report` says F. An F decline is a SILENT
  // downgrade onto the fallback, so a gate that cannot tell the two apart is green either way.
  // (v2-char-is-an-int — the control half: a Char must keep its code point in numeric position.)
  Check("v2", "v2-char-numeric-position",
        "tests/e2e/v2-char-numeric-position.sh", List(), 240000),
  // Network: one `git fetch --depth 1` per gitlink against the submodule's remote. Generous timeout
  // because a slow or flaky network must read as "this run could not tell", not as a repo defect.
  Check("scripts", "submodule-gitlinks-resolve", "tests/e2e/submodule-gitlink-resolves.sh", List(), 120000),
  // Who owns stdin — the program or the CLI. Four launcher starts, ~10 s, and it is worth them: this
  // is the only check that can see the defect at all, because until `std.os.readLine` existed no
  // lane could read stdin and therefore none could notice the CLI was eating it. It deliberately
  // pins TODAY's wrong behaviour on the tools route as well as the right one on the default lane —
  // so the S3 flip goes red here and has to be acknowledged in the same commit rather than slipping
  // through as a silent default change.
  // OPTIONAL — it pins TODAY's behaviour on the tools route (the CLI eats piped stdin), which is a
  // thing we have deliberately decided not to change yet. In the default run that assertion would
  // read as the contract; as an opt-in it reads as what it is, a recorded fact awaiting a decision.
  // Run it with SSC_SMOKE_OPTIONAL=1 before touching `Main.scala`'s stdin handling.
  Check("scripts", "stdin-ownership", "tests/e2e/stdin-belongs-to-the-program.sh", List(), 180000,
        optional = true),

  // ── tests/conformance — the product. One pass, several lanes; see `laneModule` for where the
  //    per-lane results are booked.
  // SSC_CONF_WARM_JVM=1 reuses ONE Bloop build server across the slice instead of paying a cold
  // scalac start per case, which is what `ci.yml`'s conformance job already does for the same reason.
  // MEASURED when it was added: 74.4 s -> 43.9 s locally (-41%). Same `scala-cli run`, warm compiler;
  // correctness is unchanged.
  // `--server=false` on the PARENT scala-cli, and it is a CORRECTNESS fix, not a speed one.
  // MEASURED: six `workflow_dispatch` runs of ONE commit (cf3fad14f) gave 3 green, 3 red — identical
  // code, a 50 % flake, and the log named it:
  //     Starting compilation server
  //     Exception in thread "main" java.util.concurrent.TimeoutException: Future timed out after [30 seconds]
  //       at bloop.rifle.internal.Operations$.about(Operations.scala:529)
  // ── ONE check, four lanes, ~9 s — replacing three that cost 62.9 s ────────────────────────────
  //
  //   corpus-breadth-slice     13 cases over int,js,v2      14.9 s
  //   corpus-jvm-lane           4 cases over jvm            17.8 s
  //   conformance-lanes-flag    the gate on the two above   30.2 s
  //
  // Those were sized for a world where smoke was the ONLY per-push verdict, so a corpus SAMPLE here
  // was the only corpus signal that existed. Since 2026-08-01 tier 2 (`ci.yml` on push) runs the
  // WHOLE corpus — 353 cases, every lane — on the same push, so sampling 17 of them buys nothing.
  //
  // NOT simply deleted, and this is the part worth reading before "simplifying" further:
  // `v1/runtime/backend/interpreter`, `v1/runtime/backend/jvm` and `v2` have NO OTHER CHECK in this
  // suite. Their entire smoke coverage is the corpus lanes. Removing the three outright would not
  // have dropped a duplicate, it would have gone dark on the interpreter, the JVM backend and v2 —
  // locally, before the push, where tier 2 does not exist. So: keep the breadth, drop the sampling.
  //
  // The name still starts with `corpus-` on purpose: the per-lane rollup below reads EVERY
  // `corpus-*` outcome, and that is how int/js/jvm/v2 get attributed to their own modules.
  //
  // `conformance-lanes-flag` went with them because it was never independent coverage — it existed
  // to stop the two checks above failing SILENTLY (a zero-match `--only` exits 0 over nothing; a
  // `--lanes` value could be eaten as the positional corpus dir). That protection is INLINED into
  // the new script, which asserts the case count and every lane BY NAME. Verified both ways: a
  // zero-match pattern and a case declaring fewer lanes each turn it red.
  Check("tests/conformance", "corpus-lane-breadth", "tests/e2e/smoke-lane-breadth.sh", List(), 300000)
)

// ── The module hierarchy, read rather than restated ───────────────────────────

def tsvRows(): List[List[String]] =
  readFile("tests/fixtures/modules.tsv").split("\n")
    .filter(line => line.length > 0 && !line.startsWith("#"))
    .map(line => line.split("\t"))

def declaredModules(): List[String] = tsvRows().map(row => row(0))

// run.sc prints one verdict line per lane, labelled `[INT]`, `[JS ]`, `[JS/v2]`, `[JVM]`,
// `[JVM/v2]`, `[V2 ]`. Book each against the module that owns that backend. The v2-front lanes
// (`JS/v2`, `JVM/v2`, `V2`) all belong to `v2` — the thing under test there is the front, not the
// backend it emits for.
def laneModule(label: String): String =
  val lane = label.trim
  if lane == "INT" then "v1/runtime/backend/interpreter"
  else if lane == "JS" then "v1/runtime/backend/js"
  else if lane == "JVM" then "v1/runtime/backend/jvm"
  else "v2"

// ── Formatting ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def deciseconds(nanos: Int): Int = nanos / 100000000

def showSeconds(tenths: Int): String =
  (tenths / 10).toString() + "." + (tenths % 10).toString() + "s"

def pad(text: String, width: Int): String =
  var out = text
  while out.length < width do
    out = out + " "
  out

// HEAD and TAIL, not tail alone, and this cost a real diagnosis.
//
// Two CI runs failed on `corpus-breadth-slice` (30606728752, 30606076538). What the log carried was
// eight lines of `at scala.cli.ScalaCli$.main0(...)` — pure JVM plumbing. A stack trace puts its
// CAUSE at the top and its frames at the bottom, so printing the tail printed the least informative
// end of the most important output. The failure was unreadable, and the runner had already thrown
// the rest away: nothing downstream could recover it.
//
// The first lines carry the error message; the last carry the summary. When both are shown the
// omission is stated rather than silent, because "…" with no count reads like formatting.
def headTail(text: String, keep: Int): List[String] =
  val lines = text.split("\n").filter(line => line.trim().length > 0)
  if lines.length <= keep * 2 then lines
  else
    val omitted = lines.length - keep * 2
    lines.take(keep) ++
      List("··· " + omitted.toString() + " line(s) omitted ···") ++
      lines.drop(lines.length - keep)

// ── Running ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

case class Outcome(module: String, name: String, ok: Boolean, exitCode: Int, tenths: Int,
                   stdout: String, stderr: String, skipped: Boolean = false)

// ── Host-speed probe: how fast is THIS runner, independent of what the suite contains ──────────
//
// MEASURED 2026-07-31, and it overturned the question it was meant to answer. The suite's totals had
// drifted from 194-227 s to 247-362 s against a 420 s cap, which looks like structural growth. Per
// check, between the fastest and slowest of eight green runs:
//
//     conformance-lanes-flag  68.9 -> 96.5   x1.40      launcher-input-digest  9.5 -> 20.5  x2.16
//     corpus-jvm-lane         46.4 -> 62.3   x1.34      policy-single-source   8.9 -> 17.7  x1.99
//     no-test-reaches-cli     16.7 -> 28.1   x1.68      ci-status-guard        2.9 ->  5.7  x1.97
//     …median x1.39 across 14 checks; the three that did NOT move are the ones dominated by waiting
//      rather than by CPU (build-ram-guards-guard x1.02, claim-scope-hierarchy x0.96)
//
// Nothing grew. The RUNNER is up to 1.4x slower on some days, exactly as this file's own budget
// section predicts ("uniform inflation across every check is a loaded host, not a slow suite"). A
// fixed second-count therefore measures the day as much as the suite: tight enough to catch real
// growth means flapping, loose enough not to flap means blind.
//
// The probe is process SPAWNING, not arithmetic, because that is what the suite actually spends its
// time on and what a loaded host actually slows. It is independent of the suite's contents by
// construction — the one property that matters, since deriving host speed from the checks themselves
// would hide a check that grew inside the estimate of how slow the host is.
//
// IT CHANGES NOTHING YET. It prints. The budget stays absolute until there are enough CI samples to
// set a reference honestly — this file's own history records what happens when a cap is fitted to
// local numbers (300 s, guessed from a dev machine, red on the first run).
// MILLISECONDS, and 100 spawns rather than 20. The first version reported deciseconds and did 20
// spawns: it printed `0.1s` and a 1.4x difference in host speed rounds away entirely inside that.
// An instrument that cannot resolve the thing it exists to measure is the same defect as a gate that
// cannot see the thing it guards — caught here only because the number looked implausibly round.
def hostProbeMillis(): Int =
  val startedAt = System.nanoTime()
  var i = 0
  while i < 100 do
    exec("/bin/sh", List("-c", ":"), ProcessOptions(None, Map(), Some(10000), true))
    i = i + 1
  (System.nanoTime() - startedAt) / 1000000

def runCheck(check: Check): Outcome =
  val startedAt = System.nanoTime()
  // A command that cannot be STARTED (renamed script, lost +x bit) throws out of `exec`. Left
  // uncaught it kills the runner on check 1 of 10 — non-zero, so not fail-open, but it reports one
  // unexplained line instead of nine other verdicts. Book it as this check's failure and continue.
  val result =
    try exec(check.cmd, check.argv, ProcessOptions(None, check.env, Some(check.timeoutMs), true))
    catch case e => ProcessResult("", "could not run `" + check.cmd + "`: " + e.toString(), 127)
  val elapsed = deciseconds(System.nanoTime() - startedAt)
  Outcome(check.module, check.name, result.exitCode == 0, result.exitCode, elapsed,
          result.stdout, result.stderr)

// A lane tally the harness never printed is NOT zero failures — it is an unreadable harness. The
// corpus check therefore fails when it produced no lane lines at all, rather than reporting a
// silent all-green over nothing (`feedback: pre-judging instead of comparing`).
//
// KNOWN-RED gets its OWN bucket rather than being folded into either side. It is a declared,
// self-expiring exception: counted as a pass it would hide a lane that is red on purpose, and
// counted as a failure it would make the suite permanently red. Left out of both — which is what
// the first version of this parse did — it becomes invisible, which is the worst of the three.
case class LaneTally(module: String, pass: Int, knownRed: Int, fail: Int)

def laneVerdicts(stdout: String): List[List[String]] =
  stdout.split("\n")
    .map(line => line.trim())
    .filter(line => line.startsWith("PASS [") || line.startsWith("FAIL [") ||
                    line.startsWith("DIVERGE [") || line.startsWith("KNOWN-RED ["))
    .map(line =>
      val open = line.indexOf("[")
      val close = line.indexOf("]")
      List(line.substring(0, open).trim(), line.substring(open + 1, close)))

def tallyFor(module: String, verdicts: List[List[String]]): LaneTally =
  val mine = verdicts.filter(v => laneModule(v(1)) == module)
  LaneTally(module,
            mine.filter(v => v(0) == "PASS").length,
            mine.filter(v => v(0) == "KNOWN-RED").length,
            mine.filter(v => v(0) != "PASS" && v(0) != "KNOWN-RED").length)

def showTally(tally: LaneTally): String =
  var text = tally.pass.toString() + " pass"
  if tally.knownRed > 0 then text = text + ", " + tally.knownRed.toString() + " known-red"
  if tally.fail > 0 then text = text + ", " + tally.fail.toString() + " FAIL"
  text

// ── main ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def main() =
  val argv = args
  val listOnly = argv.contains("--list")
  val budgetSeconds = envOrElse("SSC_SMOKE_BUDGET", "420").toInt()
  val modules = declaredModules()

  // The hierarchy check comes FIRST: a suite that silently drops a check because its module name
  // drifted is worse than no suite, because it still reports green.
  val unknown = suite.filter(check => !modules.contains(check.module))
  if unknown.length > 0 then
    println("smoke-ci: check(s) name a module that is not in tests/fixtures/modules.tsv:")
    unknown.foreach(check => println("  " + check.name + " -> " + check.module))
    println("The module list is the source of module identity. Add the module there, or fix the name.")
    exit(1)

  if listOnly then
    println("smoke-ci — " + suite.length.toString() + " checks, budget " +
            budgetSeconds.toString() + "s")
    suite.foreach(check =>
      println("  " + pad(check.module, 34) + check.name + "  (" + check.cmd + ")"))
    exit(0)

  val probeMillis = hostProbeMillis()
  println("smoke-ci — " + suite.length.toString() + " checks across " +
          modules.length.toString() + " declared modules, budget " +
          budgetSeconds.toString() + "s, host probe " + probeMillis.toString() +
          "ms (100 process spawns; informational — the budget is still absolute)")
  println("")

  var outcomes = List[Outcome]()
  var lastModule = ""
  suite.foreach(check =>
    if check.module != lastModule then
      println(check.module)
      lastModule = check.module
    val outcome =
      if check.optional && !runOptional then
        // `skipped = true` rather than `ok = true`: counting a check nobody ran as a pass is how a
        // suite reports a bigger number than it earned, and the verdict line below is the one thing
        // a reader takes away.
        Outcome(check.module, check.name, true, 0, 0, "", "", true)
      else runCheck(check)
    val mark =
      if check.optional && !runOptional then "  skip "
      else if outcome.ok then "  ok   " else "  FAIL "
    val suffix =
      if check.optional && !runOptional then "  (optional — SSC_SMOKE_OPTIONAL=1 to run)"
      else showSeconds(outcome.tenths)
    println(mark + pad(outcome.name, 34) + suffix)
    if !outcome.ok then
      // The exit code first: 124 is the timeout guard firing, 127 a command that could not start,
      // 137 a kill (OOM on a runner). Those three are answered by the number alone, and reading
      // them out of the output is guesswork.
      println("         | exit code " + outcome.exitCode.toString())
      headTail(outcome.stdout, 10).foreach(line => println("         | " + line))
      headTail(outcome.stderr, 10).foreach(line => println("         | " + line))
    outcomes = outcomes ++ List(outcome))

  // ── Per-module rollup: the top-level accounting ─────────────────────────────
  //
  // Two kinds of row, because the suite has two kinds of coverage. Modules with their own gates
  // are booked by check; the backend modules are booked by the corpus lane they own. Both roll up
  // into the single verdict below, which is the number anyone reading a CI log actually needs.
  println("")
  println("by module")
  modules.foreach(module =>
    val mine = outcomes.filter(o => o.module == module)
    if mine.length > 0 then
      val green = mine.filter(o => o.ok).length
      val spent = mine.map(o => o.tenths).foldLeft(0)((a, b) => a + b)
      println("  " + pad(module, 34) + green.toString() + "/" + mine.length.toString() +
              " checks   " + showSeconds(spent)))

  // EVERY corpus check, not just the first. The suite runs two of them — breadth on int/js/v2, and
  // the JVM lane on three cases — and reading only one would drop a whole lane out of the rollup
  // while still printing a confident table. Same invisibility the KNOWN-RED bucket exists to avoid.
  val corpus = outcomes.filter(o => o.name.startsWith("corpus-"))
  var laneReadable = true
  var laneFailures = 0
  if corpus.length > 0 then
    val verdicts =
      corpus.map(o => laneVerdicts(o.stdout)).foldLeft(List[List[String]]())((a, b) => a ++ b)
    if verdicts.length == 0 then
      laneReadable = false
      println("  corpus lanes: UNREADABLE — run.sc printed no `PASS [lane]` lines. The harness")
      println("  output format changed, or the run died before any case reported. Not green.")
    else
      List("v1/runtime/backend/interpreter", "v1/runtime/backend/js",
           "v1/runtime/backend/jvm", "v2").foreach(module =>
        val tally = tallyFor(module, verdicts)
        laneFailures = laneFailures + tally.fail
        if tally.pass + tally.knownRed + tally.fail > 0 then
          println("  " + pad(module, 34) + showTally(tally) + "   (corpus lanes)"))

  // ── Verdict ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  val failed  = outcomes.filter(o => !o.ok)
  val skipped = outcomes.filter(o => o.skipped)
  val ran     = outcomes.filter(o => !o.skipped)
  val totalTenths = outcomes.map(o => o.tenths).foldLeft(0)((a, b) => a + b)
  // The budget FAILS the run on CI and only WARNS locally, and that asymmetry is the point.
  //
  // Its job is to stop the PUSH PATH growing, and the push path is the GitHub job — a dedicated
  // 2-core runner. A developer machine is running several agents' builds at once; measured here at
  // load 19, `ci-status-guard` alone took 72-78 s against 4.0 s for the identical check on a runner.
  // The number being compared is a CI-sized number, so locally the check measures host contention,
  // not the suite.
  //
  // Failing on it locally had a concrete cost: an agent who cannot get a green run before pushing
  // pushes without one. That happened three times in one session — mine — each time with every
  // check green and only the total over. The timings are still printed and the warning is still
  // loud; what changes is that a loaded host no longer blocks the pre-push discipline it exists to
  // support.
  val onCi = envOrElse("CI", "") != ""
  val overBudget = totalTenths > budgetSeconds * 10

  println("")
  println("checks: " + (ran.length - failed.length).toString() + "/" +
          ran.length.toString() + " green    " +
          showSeconds(totalTenths) + " of " + budgetSeconds.toString() + "s budget")
  // Never let the skipped ones be inferred from a smaller total. The count AND the names, every
  // run — an optional check and a deleted one must not look the same in a log.
  if skipped.length > 0 then
    println("        " + skipped.length.toString() + " optional check(s) NOT run: " +
            skipped.map(o => o.name).mkString(", ") + "   (SSC_SMOKE_OPTIONAL=1 runs them)")

  if failed.length > 0 then
    println("")
    println("FAILED:")
    failed.foreach(o => println("  " + o.module + "  " + o.name))
  if overBudget then
    println("")
    if onCi then
      println("OVER BUDGET — the push path is what this suite exists to keep short. Cut a check or")
      println("make one faster; raising SSC_SMOKE_BUDGET is how the old 13.4-minute path happened.")
      println("Read the per-check timings above: one check that grew is the signal this exists for.")
    else
      println("over budget LOCALLY — reported, not failed. This budget is sized for a dedicated CI")
      println("runner; a dev host running several agents' builds inflates every check (measured at")
      println("load 19: ci-status-guard 78 s here against 4 s on a runner). If ONE check grew rather")
      println("than all of them, that is real and worth chasing before you push.")
  if !laneReadable then
    println("")
    println("CORPUS LANE OUTPUT UNREADABLE — see above.")
  // Independent of run.sc's exit code on purpose. If the harness ever prints a FAIL lane and still
  // exits 0, the check above reads green — a fail-open harness is the one failure mode a smoke
  // suite must not inherit, so the lane lines get their own say in the verdict.
  if laneFailures > 0 then
    println("")
    println("CORPUS LANE FAILURES: " + laneFailures.toString() +
            " — reported by the lane lines even though the check itself did not fail.")

  if failed.length > 0 || (overBudget && onCi) || !laneReadable || laneFailures > 0 then exit(1)
  else exit(0)
```
